INSERT INTO `lectures` VALUES (1,'compt_net','sfnmhgewrjkytrefvbv',1,32,'5th_sem_computer_net_mst.pdf','Computer Networks — Exam Solutions\nBased on Computer Networks by Andrew S. Tanenbaum | Exam-Ready 6-Mark Format\nComputer Networks (Tanenbaum) - Exam Answers\nPage 1 of 6\nQuestion 1: Discussing the limitation of \"Go-Back-N\", explain the advantage of\n\"Selective Repeat\" Sliding Window Protocol. [6 Marks]\n1. Introduction\nBoth Go-Back-N (GBN) and Selective Repeat (SR) are sliding window protocols operating at the Data Link\nLayer and Transport Layer to ensure reliable, in-order delivery of data frames over unreliable channels.\n2. Limitations of Go-Back-N (GBN) Protocol\nReceiver Window Size is 1 (Wr = 1): The receiver accepts frames only in strict sequential order. If\nframe k is lost or corrupted, all subsequent arriving frames (k+1, k+2, ...) are immediately discarded,\neven if received correctly.\nMassive Retransmission Overhead: When a timeout occurs for frame k, the sender must retransmit\nframe k plus all subsequent unacknowledged frames currently in the pipeline.\nPoor Efficiency on High BDP & Noisy Links: On links with high propagation delay or frequent bit\nerrors, retransmitting the entire window drastically degrades overall throughput.\nGBN Retransmission Example:\nSender:   [Frame 0] [Frame 1] [Frame 2 (LOST)] [Frame 3] [Frame 4]\nReceiver:    ACK 0     ACK 1     Discard!        Discard!  Discard!\nTimeout! -> Sender must retransmit: Frame 2, Frame 3, Frame 4...\n3. Advantages of Selective Repeat (SR) Protocol\nReceiver Window Size > 1 (Wr > 1): The receiver maintains a buffer of size Wr. It accepts out-of-order\nframes that fall within its window and buffers them until missing lower-numbered frames arrive.\nSelective Retransmission: Only the specific frame that was lost or corrupted is retransmitted (triggered\neither by an individual timer per frame or a Negative Acknowledgment / NAK).\nHigh Channel Utilization: Because correctly received frames are not discarded, channel throughput\nremains high even over noisy or high-latency connections.\nSelective Repeat Example:\nSender:   [Frame 0] [Frame 1] [Frame 2 (LOST)] [Frame 3] [Frame 4]\nReceiver:    ACK 0     ACK 1     (Expecting 2)    Buffered  Buffered\nNAK 2 sent -> Sender retransmits ONLY Frame 2.\n4. Mathematical Constraint (Tanenbaum\'s Rule)\nTo avoid frame ambiguity (where a receiver cannot distinguish between a fresh frame and a duplicate), the\nwindow size W in Selective Repeat must satisfy: \nWs + Wr ≤ 2m  &implies;  W ≤ 2m-1\n(where m is the number of sequence number bits)\n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \nComputer Networks (Tanenbaum) - Exam Answers\nPage 2 of 6\nQuestion 2: Compare Virtual-Circuit and Datagram Networks on the basis of\nimportant performance issues. [4/6 Marks]\n1. Conceptual Overview\nDatagram Subnets: Connectionless approach where each packet is treated independently and routed on\nan ad-hoc basis (e.g., Internet Protocol / IP).\nVirtual-Circuit Subnets: Connection-oriented approach where a logical path is pre-established before any\ndata transfer begins (e.g., MPLS, ATM, Frame Relay).\n2. Comprehensive Comparison Table\nPerformance Issue\nDatagram Subnet\nVirtual-Circuit (VC) Subnet\nCircuit Setup\nNo setup delay. Packets are sent\nimmediately without prior signaling.\nSetup delay required. Traffic cannot\nflow until the connection setup phase\ncompletes.\nAddressing Overhead\nHigh. Every packet must carry full\nsource and destination IP addresses.\nLow. Packets carry only short local\nVirtual Circuit Identifiers (VCIDs).\nState Information\nStateless. Routers keep no state\ninformation about individual\nconnections.\nStateful. Routers maintain connection\ntables for all active virtual circuits.\nRouting / Path\nDynamic. Each packet is routed\nindependently; different packets may\ntake different routes.\nFixed. Path is decided during setup;\nall packets follow the exact same\nroute.\nRouter Failures\nHighly Resilient. Packets\nautomatically bypass failed routers via\ndynamic recalculation.\nVulnerable. If a router along the circuit\nfails, all VCs through it are terminated.\nCongestion Control\nDifficult. Traffic flows unpredictably\nacross multiple paths, making\nresource reservation hard.\nEasy. Resources (bandwidth, buffer\nspace) can be reserved in advance\nduring setup.\nQuality of Service\n(QoS)\nBest-Effort. Hard to guarantee latency\nor throughput bounds per connection.\nGuaranteed. Well-suited for real-time\napplications (voice/video) via resource\nreservation.\nComputer Networks (Tanenbaum) - Exam Answers\nPage 3 of 6\nQuestion 3: Compare OSI and TCP/IP Network Architecture models in terms of\nProtocol Implementation issues. [4/6 Marks]\n1. Separation of Services, Interfaces, and Protocols\nOSI Model: Maintains a strict, explicit distinction between Services (what a layer does), Interfaces (how\nupper layers access it), and Protocols (how the layer works internally). This makes replacing protocols\ntheoretically simpler.\nTCP/IP Model: Did not clearly separate these concepts. The architecture was designed after the\nprotocols were already implemented, leading to tighter coupling and less flexibility for updates.\n2. Connection-Oriented vs. Connectionless Support\nOSI Model: Supports both Connectionless and Connection-Oriented in the Network Layer, but only\nConnection-Oriented in the Transport Layer.\nTCP/IP Model: Supports only Connectionless in the Network Layer (IP), but both modes (TCP and\nUDP) in the Transport Layer.\n3. Layer Redundancy & Overhead\nOSI Model: Defines 7 layers (including Session and Presentation). In practical software\nimplementations, Session and Presentation features are rarely implemented as distinct layers, creating\nunnecessary processing overhead and redundant error control.\nTCP/IP Model: Combines Application, Presentation, and Session into a single flexible Application\nLayer (4 layers total), optimizing performance and simplifying protocol stack implementation in operating\nsystems.\n4. The \"Bad Timing\" & \"Bad Technology\" Critique (Tanenbaum)\nOSI protocols were standardized too late. By the time OSI standards were finalized, TCP/IP was already\nheavily deployed in universities and industry (ARPANET). OSI protocols (e.g., TP4) were overly complex and\nperformed poorly compared to TCP/IP stacks.\n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \nComputer Networks (Tanenbaum) - Exam Answers\nPage 4 of 6\nQuestion 4: Data Frame Transmission with CRC Error Detection [6 Marks]\n1. Input Parameters\nOriginal Data Frame (D): 111000111000 (12 bits)\nGenerator Polynomial G(x): X5 + X4 + X2 + X + 1\n2. Generator Binary Representation\nWriting G(x) in coefficient form: 1·X5 + 1·X4 + 0·X3 + 1·X2 + 1·X1 + 1·X0\nG(x) in Binary = 110111 (Degree r = 5 check bits).\n3. Appending Zero Bits\nAppend r = 5 zero bits to data frame D: 11100011100000000\n4. Modulo-2 Division (Bitwise XOR)\n            101110011101  <-- Quotient\n          ________________\n110111 | 11100011100000000\n         110111\n         ------\n          011111\n          000000\n          ------\n           111111\n           110111\n           ------\n            010001\n            000000\n            ------\n             100011\n             110111\n             ------\n              101000\n              110111\n              ------\n               111110\n               110111\n               ------\n                010010\n                000000\n                ------\n                 100100\n                 110111\n                 ------\n                  100110\n                  110111\n                  ------\n                   100010\n                   110111\n                   ------\n                    101010\n                    110111\n                    ------\n                     11101  <-- Remainder (CRC)\nCalculated CRC Remainder: 11101\n• \n• \n• \nComputer Networks (Tanenbaum) - Exam Answers\nPage 5 of 6\nTransmitted Frame T(x): 111000111000 + 11101 = 11100011100011101\n5. Conditions for Acceptance vs. Retransmission\nCondition for Acceptance: Upon receiving frame T\'(x), the destination node divides T\'(x) by G(x) = \n110111 using modulo-2 division. If the remainder is all zeros (00000), the frame is assumed error-free\nand is ACCEPTED.\nCondition for Retransmission: If the remainder is non-zero (≠ 00000), an error occurred during\ntransmission. The destination node DISCARDS the frame and requests retransmission (via NAK or\ntimeout).\n• \n1. \n2. \nComputer Networks (Tanenbaum) - Exam Answers\nPage 6 of 6\n','Active','2026-08-13 11:19:25'),(2,'kect tes','aC BNTYTRFCAX ',1,18,'python_teacher_lecture_guide.pdf','COMPLETE SPEAKER\'S GUIDE & SESSION HANDBOOK\nMastering Python for Class 8–10 Educators\nTarget Audience: Middle & High School CS Teachers\nSession Duration: 60 Minutes\nScope: Class 8, 9 & 10 Python Syllabus\nCore Goal: Mindset Shift & Pedagogical Confidence\nSECTION 1: 2-MINUTE OPENING KEYNOTE SCRIPT\nDelivery Tip: Walk to the center of the stage/room without slides visible. Speak at a warm, steady, and encouraging pace\n(approx. 2 minutes). Use hand gestures when contrasting Java\'s complexity with Python\'s simplicity. \nVERBATIM SPEAKING MATERIAL\n\"Good morning, everyone!\nLet me take you back to December of 1989. While most of the world was celebrating Christmas, a\nDutch programmer named Guido van Rossum was stuck at home in the Netherlands looking for a\nholiday hobby project. He wanted to write a scripting language that was simple, clean, and—\nbelieve it or not—fun.\nAnd because he was a huge fan of the British comedy show Monty Python’s Flying Circus, he\nnamed his holiday side project... Python.\nWhat started as a Christmas hobby over 35 years ago is now the dominant programming language\npowering artificial intelligence, NASA space missions, and the global tech economy. But here is the\nsecret: Python wasn’t built for supercomputers. It was built for human readability. In fact, inside\nPython’s core design rules, there is a motto: \'Readability counts.\'\nNow, I know change can feel daunting. Moving from older, rigid languages like Java or C++—or\nstepping into text-based coding for the very first time in Classes 8 through 10—might feel like a\nmassive hurdle. In Java, on day one, to say \'Hello\' to a 13-year-old, you have to explain public\nstatic void main string args .\nIn Python? It’s just print(\"Hello\") .\nThink of Java like learning to fly a commercial Boeing 747 just to go down the street for groceries.\nPython is like handing your students the keys to a modern electric bicycle. It strips away the\nclutter and lets them focus on where they want to go—on logic, creativity, and problem-\nsolving.\nVariables aren\'t abstract mathematical formulas; they are just labeled cardboard boxes on a desk.\nLists are lunch boxes. Indentation is just standard paragraph formatting.\nOur job isn\'t to turn our Class 8, 9, and 10 students into software engineers overnight. Our job is to\ngive them a tool that makes them say, \'I can do this.\'\nToday, we are going to master this tool together, demystify the syllabus, and turn Python into your\nnew favorite subject to teach. Let’s dive in!\"\nPython Teacher Training Workshop | Speaker\'s Guide\nPage 1 of 4\nSECTION 2: HISTORICAL FACTS & RATIONALE (WHY PYTHON?)\nUse these talking points during your session to help teachers understand why boards and schools are\ntransitioning to Python.\nCrucial Historical Trivia to Mention\nCreated in 1989 (Released 1991): Python is actually older than Java (1995) and JavaScript (1995).\nThe BDFL: Community members gave Guido van Rossum the title of Benevolent Dictator for Life\nbecause he guided the language for nearly 30 years.\nThe Zen of Python: Built right into the language. Tell teachers to open Python and type import\nthis to reveal 19 principles like \"Simple is better than complex\" and \"Explicit is better than\nimplicit\".\nPython vs. Java / C++ (Cognitive Load Comparison)\nFeature\nJava / C++\nPython\nClassroom Benefit\nSyntax\nFriction\nHigh (Requires rigid\nboilerplate)\nLow (Clean,\npseudocode-like)\nStudents focus on logic, not\ncompiler rules.\nFirst\nProgram\n5–7 lines minimum\n1 single line\n( print() )\nZero barrier to entry on Day 1.\nTyping\nSystem\nStatic ( int x = 5; )\nDynamic ( x = 5 )\nPrevents early syntax error\nfrustration.\nDebugging\nCryptic compiler errors\nClear traceback logs\nFaster lab troubleshooting for\nteachers.\nSECTION 3: UNIQUE ANALOGIES TO SHARE WITH TEACHERS\nEncourage teachers to use these exact visual analogies in their classrooms to simplify abstract CS\nconcepts:\nVariables $ ightarrow$ Labeled Storage Boxes: A variable isn\'t a rigid math equation; it\'s a\ncardboard box with a sticky label (e.g., score ). Dynamic typing means you can swap a integer out\nfor a string anytime.\nIndentation $ ightarrow$ Paragraph Structure in Writing: Just as indenting a paragraph signals\na sub-topic in English, indenting in Python signals code that belongs inside an if block or for\nloop.\nData Types $ ightarrow$ Compartmentalized Lunch Boxes: int is a whole apple, float is a\nsliced apple, str is a written napkin note, and a list is the entire lunch box holding them all\ntogether.\nLists vs. Tuples $ ightarrow$ Whiteboard vs. Permanent Marker: A list is a whiteboard (you\ncan add, erase, or change items anytime). A tuple is written in permanent marker (immutable\nonce created).\n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \nPython Teacher Training Workshop | Speaker\'s Guide\nPage 2 of 4\nDictionaries $ ightarrow$ A Phonebook Contact List: You don\'t look up a phone number by page\nindex (0, 1, 2); you look it up by your friend\'s Name (Key $ ightarrow$ Value pair).\nSECTION 4: TOPICS TO DISCUSS (CLASS 8 TO 10 CURRICULUM)\nWalk teachers through what needs to be taught across each grade level to ensure clear progression:\nCLASS 8 Fundamentals & First Steps\nIDE & Modes: Interactive Mode (REPL) vs Script Mode ( .py files) in Thonny/IDLE.\nCharacter Set & Tokens: Keywords, Identifiers, Literals, Operators, Delimiters.\nData Types & Variables: int , float , str , bool .\nI/O Operations: input() (always returns String) and print() with sep and end\nparameters.\nBasic Math: Arithmetic operators ( + , - , * , / , // , % , ** ).\nCLASS 9 Logic Building & Flow Control\nType Conversion: Explicit ( int() , float() , str() ) vs Implicit conversion.\nRelational & Logical Operators: > , < , >= , <= , == , != , and , or , not .\nConditionals: if , if-else , and if-elif-else decision ladders.\nIteration (Loops): for loop with range(start, stop, step) and condition-based while\nloop.\nControl Transfer: break and continue statements.\nCLASS 10 Data Structures & Reusability\nString Slicing & Methods:\nstr[start:stop:step] , .upper() , .lower() , .split() , .replace() .\nLists & Operations: Indexing, slicing, and methods\n( .append() , .pop() , .remove() , .sort() ).\nTuples & Dictionaries: Immutability, key-value mappings, and basic traversal.\nUser-Defined Functions: Defining functions using def , passing parameters, and using \nreturn .\nSECTION 5: LIVE CODE DEMOS TO PRESENT\nShow teachers these classroom-ready code snippets during your technical walkthrough:\n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \nPython Teacher Training Workshop | Speaker\'s Guide\nPage 3 of 4\nDemo 1: Grade Calculator (Class 8 & 9 Level)\n# Demonstrating Input, Type Conversion, and Conditionals\nstudent_name = input(\"Enter Student Name: \")\nmarks = float(input(\"Enter Marks (0-100): \"))\nif marks >= 90:\n    grade = \"A+\"\nelif marks >= 75:\n    grade = \"A\"\nelif marks >= 50:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"Needs Improvement\"\nprint(f\"Student {student_name} scored Grade: {grade}\")\nDemo 2: Report Card & Dictionary Traversal (Class 10 Level)\n# Demonstrating Dictionaries, Loops, and Built-in Functions\nreport_card = {\n\"Math\": 92,\n\"Science\": 88,\n\"English\": 79,\n\"Computer\": 95\n}\ntotal_marks = sum(report_card.values())\navg_marks = total_marks / len(report_card)\nprint(\"--- Subject Breakdown ---\")\nfor subject, score in report_card.items():\nprint(f\"{subject}: {score}\")\nprint(f\"Average Score: {avg_marks:.2f}%\")\nSECTION 6: RECOMMENDED 60-MINUTE WORKSHOP TIMELINE\nTime\nModule Topic\nKey Focus Area\n00:00–00:10\nOpening & Mindset Shift\nDeliver 2-min script, History, Python vs. Java context.\n00:10–00:25\nSyllabus Deep-Dive\nCover Class 8–10 progression using visual analogies.\n00:25–00:40\nLive Coding & Debugging\nDemo Thonny IDE, run code, highlight student errors.\n00:40–00:50\nClassroom Pedagogy\nIntroduce PRIMM method, code tracing, non-rote exams.\n00:50–01:00\nQ&A & Resource Handout\nDistribute cheat sheets, address teacher concerns.\nPython Teacher Training Workshop | Speaker\'s Guide\nPage 4 of 4\n','Active','2026-08-17 11:46:02'),(3,'bansal banslo','wiophgfdsasdfgjklmnbvc789ihbhjokjnbvhjikmn ',1,18,'Daily_Productivity_Planner.pdf','Daily Productivity Planner\nDaily Goals\nI Finish Java/DSA lesson\nI Solve 2-4 DSA problems\nI Revise ML class\nI Revise University subjects\nI Practice Aptitude\nTime\nTask\nDone\n7:00\nWake Up\nI\n7:15-7:45\nBreakfast & Get Ready\nI\n7:45-8:20\nJava / DSA Learning\nI\n9:00-1:00\nUniversity\nI\n1:00-1:45\nLunch\nI\n1:45-2:45\nUniversity Revision\nI\n2:45-3:45\nML Revision\nI\n4:30-6:00\nML Practice\nI\n6:30-8:00\nJava + DSA Coding\nI\n8:00-8:30\nDinner\nI\n8:30-9:15\nAptitude\nI\n9:15-10:15\nDSA Practice/Revision\nI\n10:15-10:45\nPlan Tomorrow\nI\n11:30\nSleep\nI\nHabit Tracker\nHabit\nMon\nTue\nWed\nThu\nFri\nSat\nSun\nJava + DSA\nMachine Learning\nUniversity Revision\nAptitude\nExercise\n','Active','2026-08-19 11:48:47'),(4,'test_demo','machinelearning',17,18,'OOP 1 _ Introduction & Concepts - Classes, Objects, Constructors, Keywords.pdf','? OOP Foundations & Keywords\nBrief Overview\nThis note covers object‑oriented programming and was created from the OOP 1 | Introduction & Concepts - Classes,\nObjects, Constructors, Keywords YouTube video. It introduces classes and objects, explains constructors and the this\nkeyword, discusses memory allocation (heap vs. stack) and touches on garbage collection and finalization.\nKey Points\nCore OOP ideas: classes, objects, encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism.\nHow to instantiate objects with new and work with reference variables.\nRole of constructors, overloads, and the this keyword in initializing state.\nBasics of Java memory model, including heap/stack, final, and garbage collection.\n? OOP Syllabus Overview\n? Topic\n? Brief Description\nPackages\nGrouping related classes.\nStatic / Non‑static\nClass‑level vs. instance‑level members.\nSingleton Class\nEnsures a single instance.\nProperties\nAttributes of a class (e.g., roll number, name).\nPolymorphism\nSame interface, multiple implementations.\nOverloading / Overriding\nSame method name with different signatures vs.\nredefining a superclass method.\nConstructor & Special Methods\nObject initialization and lifecycle hooks (this, final,\nfinalize).\nInheritance Types\nSingle, multiple (via interfaces), hierarchical, etc.\nEncapsulation\nHiding internal state behind methods.\nAbstraction\nExposing only essential features.\nAccess Control\npublic, protected, private, package‑private.\nObject Class & Cloning\nRoot of all classes; copying objects.\nInterfaces & Abstract Classes\nContracts vs. partial implementations.\nGenerics\nType‑safe collections and methods.\nException Handling\ntry, catch, finally, custom exceptions.\nCollection Framework\nLists, sets, maps, queues.\nLambda Expressions\nFunctional style for interfaces.\nEnums\nFixed set of constants.\nFile Handling\nReading/writing files.\nFast I/O\nBuffered streams for performance.\n?️ Classes and Objects\nClass – A named group of properties (fields) and functions (methods) that defines a new data type.\nObject – An instance of a class; the concrete entity that occupies memory.\nBy convention, class names start with a capital letter (e.g., Student).\nReal‑world analogies\nCar → Car class defines properties like engine, price, seats. Specific models (BMW, Audi, Ferrari) are\nobjects with different values.\nHuman → Human class defines properties such as arms, legs, eyes. Each person is an object of that\nclass.\n?️ Instance Variables & the Dot Operator\nInstance Variable – A variable declared inside a class (but outside methods/constructors) that belongs to each\nobject of that class.\nAccess syntax: objectReference.instanceVariable\nExample: student1.rollNumber returns the roll number of student1.\nThe dot (.) acts as a separator linking a reference variable with one of its instance variables or methods.\n? Creating Objects\nnew Operator – Dynamically allocates memory at run time, creates an object on the heap, and returns a reference\nto it.\nStudent student1 = new Student();   // allocate a Student object, store its reference in student1\nReference Variable (e.g., student1) lives in stack memory; it holds the address of the actual object on the\nheap.\nBefore new is used, a reference variable holds the default value null (no object assigned). Attempting to use it\nresults in a compile‑time error: “variable might not have been initialized.”\n? Object Properties\nIdentity Example: Two reference variables pointing to the same Student object will see changes made\nthrough either reference.\nProperty\nMeaning\nState\nCurrent values of the object\'s instance variables (e.g.,\nrollNumber = 14).\nIdentity\nThe unique location of the object in memory; two\nreferences can point to the same object.\nBehavior\nThe actions the object can perform, defined by its\nmethods (e.g., greet() for a Human).\n? Packages (Brief)\nPackages organize classes into namespaces, preventing naming conflicts and providing access control.\nSyntax (mentioned later): package com.example.myapp;\n?️ Heap vs. Stack Memory\nHeap – Region of memory where objects are allocated at runtime.\nStack – Region of memory that stores reference variables and primitive values created inside methods.\nReference variables (e.g., student1) live on the stack and hold the address of an object residing in the heap.\nThe actual memory address is not accessible in Java; this protects against pointer arithmetic errors.\n⚡ Dynamic Memory Allocation & the new Operator\nDynamic memory allocation – Allocation of memory during program execution (runtime) rather than at compile\ntime.\nCode on the left side of = (declaration) is processed at compile time.\nThe expression on the right side (new Student()) is executed at runtime, causing the JVM to allocate space\non the heap and return a reference.\nStudent student1 = new Student();   // heap allocation, stack reference\nThe new keyword creates an object and initializes its memory with default values (zero, null, 0.0, etc.).\n? Reference Variables and Default Values\nReference variable – A variable that stores a reference (address) to an object in the heap.\nBefore new is invoked, a reference variable holds null.\nAfter allocation, the object’s fields contain default values until explicitly set:\nLocation\nStores\nLifetime\nStack\nPrimitive values, reference variables\nCreated when a method is entered,\ndiscarded when it exits\nHeap\nObjects (new Student())\nExists until no reference points to it\n(garbage‑collected)\nData type\nDefault value\nint / byte / short / long\n0\nfloat / double\n0.0\nboolean\nfalse\nchar\n\'\\u0000\' (null character)\nObject types (String, custom classes)\nnull\nSystem.out.println(Kunal.rollNumber); // prints 0\nSystem.out.println(Kunal.name);       // prints null\nSystem.out.println(Kunal.marks);      // prints 0.0\n? The this Keyword\nthis – A reference to the current object whose members (fields, methods) are being accessed.\nUsed inside constructors and instance methods to disambiguate between parameters and instance fields.\nEquivalent to Python’s self; automatically replaced with the name of the reference variable (Kunal, Rahul, …) at\nruntime.\npublic Student(int rollNumber, String name, float marks) {\n    this.rollNumber = rollNumber; // instance field ← parameter\n    this.name       = name;\n    this.marks      = marks;\n}\n?️ Constructors\nConstructor – A special method‑like block that runs once when an object is created, initializing its state.\nDefault (no‑arg) constructor: Provided implicitly if no constructor is declared; assigns default values.\nParameterized constructor: Defined by the programmer to require initial values.\nKey points\nNo return type (not even void).\nName exactly matches the class name.\nCan be overloaded (multiple constructors with different parameter lists).\npublic class Student {\n    int rollNumber;\n    String name;\n    float marks;\n    // Default constructor (implicit if omitted)\n    public Student() { }\n    // Parameterized constructor\n    public Student(int rollNumber, String name, float marks) {\n        this.rollNumber = rollNumber;\n        this.name       = name;\n        this.marks      = marks;\n    }\n}\n? Example: Student Class with Methods\npublic class Student {\n    int rollNumber;\n    String name;\n    float marks;\n    // Parameterized constructor\n    public Student(int rollNumber, String name, float marks) {\n        this.rollNumber = rollNumber;\n        this.name       = name;\n        this.marks      = marks;\n    }\n    // Instance method: greeting\n    public void greeting() {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, my name is \" + this.name);\n    }\n    // Instance method: changeName\n    public void changeName(String newName) {\n        this.name = newName;\n    }\n}\nUsage\nStudent Kunal = new Student(13, \"Kunal Kushwaha\", 88.5f);\nKunal.greeting();          // → Hello, my name is Kunal Kushwaha\nKunal.changeName(\"Shoe Lover\");\nKunal.greeting();          // → Hello, my name is Shoe Lover\nThe constructor sets all fields at object creation, eliminating repetitive assignments (Kunal.rollNumber = 13;\netc.).\nInside greeting() and changeName(), this.name refers to the specific object\'s name field, ensuring each\ninstance behaves independently.\n? Using the this Keyword\nDefinition – this is a reference to the current object whose members are being accessed.\nDisambiguates between instance variables and parameters that share the same name.\nExample when parameter names match fields:\npublic Student(int rollNumber, String name, float marks) {\n    this.rollNumber = rollNumber;\n    this.name       = name;\n    this.marks      = marks;\n}\nIf the parameter name differs, this is optional but often kept for readability:\npublic Student(int rno, String n, float m) {\n    rollNumber = rno;   // same effect as this.rollNumber = rno;\n    name       = n;\n    marks      = m;\n}\nInside a method, this behaves the same way, e.g., this.greeting() refers to the method of the current object.\n? Constructor Overloading & this() Calls\nDefinition – Constructor overloading provides multiple ways to instantiate an object by defining several constructors\nwith different parameter lists.\nNo‑arg constructor (default) can be explicitly defined:\npublic Student() { }\nParameterized constructors require arguments:\npublic Student(int rollNumber, String name, float marks) {\n    this.rollNumber = rollNumber;\n    this.name       = name;\n    this.marks      = marks;\n}\nOne constructor may delegate to another using this(...):\npublic Student() {\n    this(0, \"Default\", 0.0f);   // calls the three‑argument constructor\n}\nIf a matching constructor does not exist, the compiler reports an error (e.g., trying to instantiate with two\narguments when only one‑ and three‑argument versions are defined).\n? Wrapper Classes for Primitive Types\nDefinition – Wrapper classes encapsulate primitive values inside objects, giving access to methods and enabling use\nin collections.\nPrimitive\nWrapper Class\nint\nInteger\nfloat\nFloat\ndouble\nDouble\nchar\nCharacter\nint a = 10;                     // primitive, stored on the stack\nInteger num = new Integer(45);  // object, stored on the heap\nWrapper objects provide methods such as intValue(), compareTo(), toString(), etc.\nAutoboxing allows implicit conversion: Integer n = 5; is compiled to new Integer(5).\n? Reference Assignment – Shared Objects\nDefinition – Assigning one reference variable to another copies the reference, not the object itself.\nStudent s1 = new Student(13, \"Kunal\", 85.4f);\nStudent s2 = s1;        // s2 points to the same object as s1\ns1.name = \"Changed\";\nSystem.out.println(s2.name);   // prints \"Changed\"\nNo additional memory is allocated; both variables refer to the same heap object.\nMutating the object through any reference is reflected when accessed through other references.\n?️ The final Keyword\nDefinition – final makes a variable, method, or class immutable in the sense that it cannot be reassigned or\noverridden.\nFinal Variables (Constants)\nMust be initialized at the point of declaration or in a constructor.\nConvention: name in UPPER_SNAKE_CASE.\nfinal int BONUS_PERCENT = 2;   // cannot be changed later\nFinal Fields in Classes\nFor primitive fields, the value cannot change after initialization.\nFor reference fields, the reference cannot be changed, but the object\'s internal state can still be modified.\nclass Example {\n    final Student fixedStudent = new Student(1, \"Alice\", 90.0f);\n    // fixedStudent = new Student(...); // compilation error\n    void updateName(String newName) {\n        fixedStudent.name = newName;   // allowed: modifies object\'s state\nboolean\nBoolean\nbyte\nByte\nshort\nShort\nlong\nLong\n    }\n}\nFinal Classes\nA class declared final cannot be subclassed.\nExample: public final class Integer { ... } (the wrapper classes are final).\n? Pass‑by‑Value vs. Pass‑by‑Reference\nDefinition – Java always uses pass‑by‑value; for primitives the value is the actual data, for objects the value is the\nreference.\nPrimitive example – swapping inside a method does not affect the original variables:\nvoid swap(int a, int b) { int temp = a; a = b; b = temp; }\n// calling swap(x, y) leaves x and y unchanged\nObject example – the reference is passed by value; both the caller and callee refer to the same object, but\nreassigning the parameter does not affect the caller\'s reference.\nvoid reassign(Student s) { s = new Student(); } // caller\'s reference unchanged\nWrapper objects (Integer, Float, etc.) are immutable; even though they are objects, their state cannot be\nchanged, so swapping their references behaves like primitive swapping.\n? Summary of Key Interactions\n?️ Garbage Collection Overview\nConcept\nHow it relates to earlier material\nthis\nConnects constructor parameters to the object\'s fields\ndiscussed in Instance Variables.\nConstructor overloading\nExtends the Constructor topic by allowing multiple\ninitialization signatures.\nWrapper classes\nProvides an object view of primitive data types,\nbridging the gap between stack‑only primitives and\nheap‑based objects.\nReference assignment\nReinforces the Heap vs. Stack relationship; multiple\nreferences can point to a single heap object.\nfinal\nAdds immutability rules to Access Control and Object\nState concepts.\nPass‑by‑value\nClarifies why method arguments behave the way they\ndo, complementing earlier notes on Method Calls.\nGarbage Collection – An automatic process in Java that reclaims memory occupied by objects that are no longer\nreachable from any live reference variable.\nOperates on the heap where objects reside.\nRuns implicitly; developers cannot invoke it directly to free a specific object.\nWhen an object becomes unreachable (e.g., a reference variable is reassigned), the collector may later reclaim\nits memory.\n? Finalization & the finalize Method\nFinalization – A mechanism that lets a class specify actions to be performed just before the garbage collector\nactually removes the object.\nImplemented by overriding the finalize() method inherited from java.lang.Object.\nHistorically used for resource cleanup (e.g., closing a file stream).\nDeprecated in recent Java versions; newer APIs (e.g., try‑with‑resources) are preferred.\nHow finalize Works\nThe JVM identifies an unreachable object.\nBefore reclaiming its memory, it places the object in a finalization queue.\nThe finalize() method of that object is invoked once by a dedicated finalizer thread.\nAfter finalize() completes, the object becomes eligible for ordinary garbage collection.\nExample finalize Implementation\npublic class A {\n    @Override\n    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {\n        System.out.println(\"Object of class A is being destroyed\");\n        super.finalize();   // optional: invoke Object\'s finalize\n    }\n}\n@Override signals that the method replaces Object.finalize().\nthrows Throwable is required by the method signature (covered later).\nLimitations & Best Practices\nNo guarantee of timing – finalize() may run long after the object becomes unreachable, or not at all before\nthe JVM exits.\nPerformance cost – Objects with finalizers are processed slower because they survive at least one extra GC\ncycle.\nUnreliable for critical resources – Use explicit cleanup (close(), try‑with‑resources) instead of relying on\nfinalization.\nCannot be called manually – Attempting obj.finalize() throws an exception; the method is intended solely for\nthe GC.\n? Triggering Garbage Collection (Demonstration)\nA loop creates many instances of A using a single reference variable:\nfor (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\n    obj = new A();   // each iteration overwrites the previous reference\n}\nEach iteration discards the previous object, making it unreachable.\nWhen memory pressure rises, the garbage collector runs and invokes finalize() for the reclaimed objects,\nprinting “Object of class A is being destroyed.”\nObservations from the Demo\nWith a small number of objects, the GC may not run, so no output appears.\nIncreasing the allocation count forces the GC to act, and the finalizer messages become visible.\nThe same reference variable (obj) can point to many different objects over time; only the most recent object\nremains reachable.\n? Why Manual Destruction Is Not Allowed\nManual object destruction – The act of explicitly freeing an object\'s memory by the programmer.\nIn Java, this is prohibited; only the garbage collector decides when an object’s memory is reclaimed.\nPrevents pointer arithmetic errors and dangling references.\nGuarantees type safety and memory safety across the JVM.\n? Interview‑Relevant Points\nTopic\nTypical Question\nKey Takeaway\nGarbage Collection\nHow does Java know when to free an\nobject?\nObjects become unreachable; GC\nreclaims them automatically.\nfinalize method\nWhat is the purpose of finalize() and\nwhy is it deprecated?\nAllows one‑time cleanup before GC;\ndeprecated due to unpredictability\nand performance issues.\nResource Management\nWhat should you use instead of\nfinalize() for closing files?\ntry‑with‑resources or explicit close()\ncalls.\nFinalizer Thread\nCan you force the finalizer to run?\nNo; you can suggest GC\n(System.gc()), but finalizer execution\nremains nondeterministic.\n','Active','2026-08-19 11:53:29'),(5,'nanananana','zxcvbnmklkjhgfdsaqwertyuiop',1,18,'iterndata.pdf','company name\nE-mail\nDesignation\nmobile\ncity\nTbo.tek\nraghav.gadihoke@tbo.com\nhr\n+91 81784 14487\ngurugram\nDeloitte\nvinapandey@deloitte.com\nhr\n+91 93540 86689\ngurgaon\nDeloitte\nmedhsingh@deloitte.com\nsenior analyst\n+91 96501 11589\nnew delhi\nDeloitte\nmsultana@deloitte.com\nAM\n+1 212-492-4000\nnew delhi\nEy\nkriti.saxena@in.ey.com\ntalent aqquisition\n+91 97119 70373\nnew delhi\nEy\nrakhi.sharma@ey.com\nHR consultant\n+91 89292 25336\nnew delhi\nEy\nshikha.sikri@gds.ey.com\ntalent aqquisition mana+91 12464 43989\nGurgaon\nEy\nshikha.sikri@gmail.com\ntalent aqquisition mana+91 98882 21075\nGurgaon\nKPMG\nsalonik@kpmg.com\nanalyst\n+91 88009 31661\nFaridabad\nKPMG\nanamikakumari6@kpmg.com\ntalen aqquisition\n+91 80951 66766\nnew delhi\nname\nvinay pandey\nMedhatushti\nmunira sultana\nkriti saxena\nrakhi sharma\nshikha sikri\nshikha sikri\nSALONI KALRA\nanamika kumari\n','Active','2026-08-20 11:03:07'),(6,'vbnm','cvbnkjhbnhnmjmnnbjm',1,18,'collegeadmissions.gndu.ac.in_StudentArea_printRegistrationForm.aspx_ptype=R.pdf','REGISTRATION FORM\nYour Student id is : 2026010006285\nSign ID :\n    \nPERSONAL DETAILS\n \n  (In English)\n(In Punjabi)\n  Candidate Name MUSKAN SHARMA\nmuskwnSrmw\nFather Name SURAJ PARKASH\nsUrjpRkwS\nMother Name MUDHU BALA\nbwlw\nGender : Female\nDate of Birth 28-8-2008\nBlood Group B+\nCORRESPONDENCE ADDRESS                                         \nAddress 163/6KATRA HARI SINGH JANGI SHIVALA,AMRITSAR,PUNJAB-143001  \nCountry India\n   \nPERMANENT ADDRESS                                         \nAddress 163/6KATRA HARI SINGH JANGI SHIVALA,AMRITSAR,PUNJAB-143001   \nCountry India\n \nACADEMIC DETAILS                               \nBoard name : Punjab School Education Board\nABC ID 678091398336\nUniv. Registration No.\nPrevious Class 10+2\nRoll No 2026107488\nPassing Year 2026\nMonth April\nResult P (Grade 8.5)\nMarks (%) 428/500 Percentage =85.6%\nOTHER DETAILS                            \nCategory GENERAL\nReligion HINDU\nAadhar 578613284864\nEmail id muskan18880@gmail.com\nUrban/Rural Urban\nPhysically Challenged No\nIt is hereby declared that the information and particulars furnished above are true and correct to the best of my/our\nknowledge and belief and nothing has been concealed.\n   \n \n   \n \nSignature\n   \nPrinted on 18/05/2026\nPrint\nContinue to Campus Admission\nLogin for Private/College Admission/Other Services\n5/18/26, 8:25 PM\ncollegeadmissions.gndu.ac.in/StudentArea/printRegistrationForm.aspx?ptype=R\nhttps://collegeadmissions.gndu.ac.in/StudentArea/printRegistrationForm.aspx?ptype=R\n1/1\n','Active','2026-08-20 11:21:06'),(7,'mlmlml','vcvhlusdhifavns',1,18,'python_teacher_lecture_guide.pdf','COMPLETE SPEAKER\'S GUIDE & SESSION HANDBOOK\nMastering Python for Class 8–10 Educators\nTarget Audience: Middle & High School CS Teachers\nSession Duration: 60 Minutes\nScope: Class 8, 9 & 10 Python Syllabus\nCore Goal: Mindset Shift & Pedagogical Confidence\nSECTION 1: 2-MINUTE OPENING KEYNOTE SCRIPT\nDelivery Tip: Walk to the center of the stage/room without slides visible. Speak at a warm, steady, and encouraging pace\n(approx. 2 minutes). Use hand gestures when contrasting Java\'s complexity with Python\'s simplicity. \nVERBATIM SPEAKING MATERIAL\n\"Good morning, everyone!\nLet me take you back to December of 1989. While most of the world was celebrating Christmas, a\nDutch programmer named Guido van Rossum was stuck at home in the Netherlands looking for a\nholiday hobby project. He wanted to write a scripting language that was simple, clean, and—\nbelieve it or not—fun.\nAnd because he was a huge fan of the British comedy show Monty Python’s Flying Circus, he\nnamed his holiday side project... Python.\nWhat started as a Christmas hobby over 35 years ago is now the dominant programming language\npowering artificial intelligence, NASA space missions, and the global tech economy. But here is the\nsecret: Python wasn’t built for supercomputers. It was built for human readability. In fact, inside\nPython’s core design rules, there is a motto: \'Readability counts.\'\nNow, I know change can feel daunting. Moving from older, rigid languages like Java or C++—or\nstepping into text-based coding for the very first time in Classes 8 through 10—might feel like a\nmassive hurdle. In Java, on day one, to say \'Hello\' to a 13-year-old, you have to explain public\nstatic void main string args .\nIn Python? It’s just print(\"Hello\") .\nThink of Java like learning to fly a commercial Boeing 747 just to go down the street for groceries.\nPython is like handing your students the keys to a modern electric bicycle. It strips away the\nclutter and lets them focus on where they want to go—on logic, creativity, and problem-\nsolving.\nVariables aren\'t abstract mathematical formulas; they are just labeled cardboard boxes on a desk.\nLists are lunch boxes. Indentation is just standard paragraph formatting.\nOur job isn\'t to turn our Class 8, 9, and 10 students into software engineers overnight. Our job is to\ngive them a tool that makes them say, \'I can do this.\'\nToday, we are going to master this tool together, demystify the syllabus, and turn Python into your\nnew favorite subject to teach. Let’s dive in!\"\nPython Teacher Training Workshop | Speaker\'s Guide\nPage 1 of 4\nSECTION 2: HISTORICAL FACTS & RATIONALE (WHY PYTHON?)\nUse these talking points during your session to help teachers understand why boards and schools are\ntransitioning to Python.\nCrucial Historical Trivia to Mention\nCreated in 1989 (Released 1991): Python is actually older than Java (1995) and JavaScript (1995).\nThe BDFL: Community members gave Guido van Rossum the title of Benevolent Dictator for Life\nbecause he guided the language for nearly 30 years.\nThe Zen of Python: Built right into the language. Tell teachers to open Python and type import\nthis to reveal 19 principles like \"Simple is better than complex\" and \"Explicit is better than\nimplicit\".\nPython vs. Java / C++ (Cognitive Load Comparison)\nFeature\nJava / C++\nPython\nClassroom Benefit\nSyntax\nFriction\nHigh (Requires rigid\nboilerplate)\nLow (Clean,\npseudocode-like)\nStudents focus on logic, not\ncompiler rules.\nFirst\nProgram\n5–7 lines minimum\n1 single line\n( print() )\nZero barrier to entry on Day 1.\nTyping\nSystem\nStatic ( int x = 5; )\nDynamic ( x = 5 )\nPrevents early syntax error\nfrustration.\nDebugging\nCryptic compiler errors\nClear traceback logs\nFaster lab troubleshooting for\nteachers.\nSECTION 3: UNIQUE ANALOGIES TO SHARE WITH TEACHERS\nEncourage teachers to use these exact visual analogies in their classrooms to simplify abstract CS\nconcepts:\nVariables $ ightarrow$ Labeled Storage Boxes: A variable isn\'t a rigid math equation; it\'s a\ncardboard box with a sticky label (e.g., score ). Dynamic typing means you can swap a integer out\nfor a string anytime.\nIndentation $ ightarrow$ Paragraph Structure in Writing: Just as indenting a paragraph signals\na sub-topic in English, indenting in Python signals code that belongs inside an if block or for\nloop.\nData Types $ ightarrow$ Compartmentalized Lunch Boxes: int is a whole apple, float is a\nsliced apple, str is a written napkin note, and a list is the entire lunch box holding them all\ntogether.\nLists vs. Tuples $ ightarrow$ Whiteboard vs. Permanent Marker: A list is a whiteboard (you\ncan add, erase, or change items anytime). A tuple is written in permanent marker (immutable\nonce created).\n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \nPython Teacher Training Workshop | Speaker\'s Guide\nPage 2 of 4\nDictionaries $ ightarrow$ A Phonebook Contact List: You don\'t look up a phone number by page\nindex (0, 1, 2); you look it up by your friend\'s Name (Key $ ightarrow$ Value pair).\nSECTION 4: TOPICS TO DISCUSS (CLASS 8 TO 10 CURRICULUM)\nWalk teachers through what needs to be taught across each grade level to ensure clear progression:\nCLASS 8 Fundamentals & First Steps\nIDE & Modes: Interactive Mode (REPL) vs Script Mode ( .py files) in Thonny/IDLE.\nCharacter Set & Tokens: Keywords, Identifiers, Literals, Operators, Delimiters.\nData Types & Variables: int , float , str , bool .\nI/O Operations: input() (always returns String) and print() with sep and end\nparameters.\nBasic Math: Arithmetic operators ( + , - , * , / , // , % , ** ).\nCLASS 9 Logic Building & Flow Control\nType Conversion: Explicit ( int() , float() , str() ) vs Implicit conversion.\nRelational & Logical Operators: > , < , >= , <= , == , != , and , or , not .\nConditionals: if , if-else , and if-elif-else decision ladders.\nIteration (Loops): for loop with range(start, stop, step) and condition-based while\nloop.\nControl Transfer: break and continue statements.\nCLASS 10 Data Structures & Reusability\nString Slicing & Methods:\nstr[start:stop:step] , .upper() , .lower() , .split() , .replace() .\nLists & Operations: Indexing, slicing, and methods\n( .append() , .pop() , .remove() , .sort() ).\nTuples & Dictionaries: Immutability, key-value mappings, and basic traversal.\nUser-Defined Functions: Defining functions using def , passing parameters, and using \nreturn .\nSECTION 5: LIVE CODE DEMOS TO PRESENT\nShow teachers these classroom-ready code snippets during your technical walkthrough:\n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \nPython Teacher Training Workshop | Speaker\'s Guide\nPage 3 of 4\nDemo 1: Grade Calculator (Class 8 & 9 Level)\n# Demonstrating Input, Type Conversion, and Conditionals\nstudent_name = input(\"Enter Student Name: \")\nmarks = float(input(\"Enter Marks (0-100): \"))\nif marks >= 90:\n    grade = \"A+\"\nelif marks >= 75:\n    grade = \"A\"\nelif marks >= 50:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"Needs Improvement\"\nprint(f\"Student {student_name} scored Grade: {grade}\")\nDemo 2: Report Card & Dictionary Traversal (Class 10 Level)\n# Demonstrating Dictionaries, Loops, and Built-in Functions\nreport_card = {\n\"Math\": 92,\n\"Science\": 88,\n\"English\": 79,\n\"Computer\": 95\n}\ntotal_marks = sum(report_card.values())\navg_marks = total_marks / len(report_card)\nprint(\"--- Subject Breakdown ---\")\nfor subject, score in report_card.items():\nprint(f\"{subject}: {score}\")\nprint(f\"Average Score: {avg_marks:.2f}%\")\nSECTION 6: RECOMMENDED 60-MINUTE WORKSHOP TIMELINE\nTime\nModule Topic\nKey Focus Area\n00:00–00:10\nOpening & Mindset Shift\nDeliver 2-min script, History, Python vs. Java context.\n00:10–00:25\nSyllabus Deep-Dive\nCover Class 8–10 progression using visual analogies.\n00:25–00:40\nLive Coding & Debugging\nDemo Thonny IDE, run code, highlight student errors.\n00:40–00:50\nClassroom Pedagogy\nIntroduce PRIMM method, code tracing, non-rote exams.\n00:50–01:00\nQ&A & Resource Handout\nDistribute cheat sheets, address teacher concerns.\nPython Teacher Training Workshop | Speaker\'s Guide\nPage 4 of 4\n','Active','2026-08-20 12:06:34'),(8,'Test','testststtss',27,35,'04 Radial Basis Function Neural Netowork_new.pdf','I N T R O D U C T I O N T O N E U R A L N E T W O R K S\nRadial Basis Function\nNetworks\nA local, two-stage alternative to the Multi-Layer Perceptron — built on\nfunction approximation and exact interpolation.\nR E C A P · T H E A N N F A M I L Y\nTwo Ways to Approximate a Function\nMulti-Layer Perceptron (MLP)\nHidden units use sigmoid activations. Each neuron divides the input space with a \nhyperplane. Neurons stay active over large, overlapping regions — a global\napproximator. Trained end-to-end with backpropagation.\nRadial Basis Function (RBF) Network\nHidden units respond only to inputs near a centre — a local approximator. Trained \nin two fast, largely non-iterative stages.\nToday\'s focus: RBF networks were built from function-approximation theory \n— not biology — so we start from interpolation, not neurons.\nMLP hidden unit: sigmoid (global)\nRBF hidden unit: Gaussian (local)\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n2\nD E F I N I T I O N\nWhat Is a Radial Basis Function Network?\nTwo-layer, feed-forward\nJust one hidden layer of basis-function units, plus a \nlinear output layer.\nRadial hidden units\nEach hidden node implements a radial basis function \n(e.g. Gaussian) of distance from a centre.\nLinear output\nOutput nodes are simple weighted-sum (linear) units, \nexactly as in an MLP.\nTwo-stage training\nInput→hidden weights are set first; hidden→output \nweights are solved second.\nVery fast training\nBecause the last stage is linear, it can be solved \ndirectly — no lengthy gradient descent.\nExcellent at interpolation\nBuilt directly from function-approximation / \ninterpolation theory.\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n3\nS T R U C T U R E\nNetwork Architecture at a Glance\nINPUT LAYER\nHIDDEN LAYER (RBF)\nOUTPUT LAYER\nx1\nx2\nx3\nφ1\nφ2\nφ3\nφ4\nΣ\ny(x)\nRaw feature\nvalues\nEach unit fires when x is near\nits centre μj (width σj)\nWeighted sum\nwkj · φj(x)\nOnly one hidden layer\nUnlike an MLP, depth never grows — RBF \nnets stay at exactly two computational \nlayers, input→hidden and \nhidden→output.\nGrey lines: every input reaches every hidden \nunit.\nTeal lines: every hidden unit feeds the \noutput, weighted by wkj.\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n4\nF O U N D A T I O N S\nFirst, What Does “Interpolation” Mean?\nInterpolation is finding a function that reconstructs values exactly at every known sample — and estimates sensible values in between. It\'s the classic approach \nwhenever you have a small set of trustworthy, noise-free measurements.\nINTERPOLATION\n✓Curve passes through every point exactly\n✓Best for sparse, noise-free data\n✓This is the RBF network\'s origin\nAPPROXIMATION / REGRESSION\n✓Curve stays close but skips exact points\n✓Best for noisy, redundant data\n✓This is what backprop-trained MLPs do\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n5\nF O U N D A T I O N S\nHow It Works: A Sum of Weighted Bumps\nPlace one basis function at every data point, scale each by a weight, and add them up. Pick the weights so the sum lands exactly on each target — that\'s the whole \nidea.\nf(x)  =  sum of all bumps\nindividual weighted bumps  wj · φj(x)\ndata point (exact hit)\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n6\nF O U N D A T I O N S\nFormalising Exact Interpolation\nGiven N data points (xᵖ, tᵖ), the goal is a function f(x) satisfying:\nf(xᵖ) = tᵖ   for  p = 1, ..., N\nRBF places one basis function on every data point, then blends them with a linear \ncombination — exactly what you just saw on the previous slide:\nf(x) = Σₙ wp · φ( ‖x − xᵖ‖ )\nφ(·)  a chosen non-linear function of distance\nwp  the weight on the p-th basis function — solved so f hits every target exactly\nWhy this framing matters\n•  One basis function per data point means N unknown weights \nand N equations — a square, solvable system.\n•  The function is a sum, so it\'s linear in the weights — even \nthough φ itself is non-linear.\n•  That linearity is what makes the next step — solving for the \nweights — a simple matrix inversion instead of an iterative \nsearch.\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n7\nF O U N D A T I O N S\nSolving for the Weights\nSubstituting every training point into f(x) gives one linear equation per data point:\nΣq wq φ( ‖xᵖ − xᵗ‖ )  =  tᵖ\nIn matrix form, with Φpq = φ(‖xq − xp‖):\nΦ w = t          w = Φ⁻¹ t\n✓Φ is provably non-singular (invertible) for many common φ, as long as the data \npoints are distinct.\n✓Once w is known, f(x) is a smooth, continuous, differentiable surface passing \nexactly through every point.\nΦ\nN × N basis-function\nactivations\n×\nw\n=\nt\ntargets\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n8\nF O U N D A T I O N S\nThis Is Also a Neural Network\nRedraw the interpolation equation as a network, and the resemblance to an ANN is exact — just with unusual weights:\nInput → hidden “weights” are simply the training inputs xᵖ themselves — nothing is \nlearned here.\nHidden → output weights are the solved values Σq Φ⁻¹pq tq — computed once from Φ⁻¹ and the \ntargets.\nx1\nx2\nx3\nInputs\nφp\nφp\nφp\nφp\nφp\nHidden: one unit\nper TRAINING POINT\nΣ\ny(x)\nOutput\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n9\nB U I L D I N G B L O C K S\nCommonly Used Radial Basis Functions φ(r)\nEmpirically, the exact shape of φ matters less than you\'d expect — but locality (below) matters a lot.\nFunction\nFormula\nBehaviour\nGaussian\nφ(r) = exp( − r² / 2σ² )\nLocalised — the standard choice\nMulti-quadric\nφ(r) = (r² + σ²)^(1/2)\nGrows with r\nInverse multi-quadric\nφ(r) = (r² + σ²)^(−1/2)\nLocalised\nThin plate spline\nφ(r) = r² ln(r)\nGrows with r\nCubic\nφ(r) = r³\nGrows with r\nLinear\nφ(r) = r\nPiecewise-linear interpolation\nWe\'ll focus on the Gaussian from here on — it\'s localised and has convenient analytic properties.\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n10\nB U I L D I N G B L O C K S\nLocalised vs. Global Basis Functions\nLocalised:  φ(r) → 0  as  r → ∞\ne.g. Gaussian, Inverse multi-quadric — activation fades to zero far from the centre.\nUnbounded:  φ(r) → ∞  as  r → ∞\ne.g. Multi-quadric, Thin plate spline, Cubic, Linear — still non-linear in x, but never \nfades out.\nWhy locality matters for a neural network: a localised unit only “fires” for inputs near its centre. That gives cleaner, more interpretable feature \ndetectors — which is why Gaussian RBFs are the default in practice.\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n11\nL I M I T A T I O N S\nTwo Problems With Exact Interpolation\n1. Performs poorly with noisy data\nForcing the curve through every noisy point produces a highly \noscillatory function — the same generalisation problem seen with \nover-fit MLPs.\n2. Not computationally efficient\nOne hidden unit is needed per training pattern. Unlike MLPs — where \nmore data generally helps — here more data means a bigger, slower \nnetwork.\nN training points  →  N hidden units\nGrows linearly — costly for large datasets.\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n12\nM A K I N G I T P R A C T I C A L\nImproving RBF Networks: Four Changes\n1\nFewer hidden units\nM basis functions need not equal N data points — in practice \nM ≪N.\n2\nLearned centres\nCentres μj no longer have to sit exactly on training inputs; a \ntraining algorithm places them.\n3\nLearned widths\nWidths σj can vary per unit and are also determined by \ntraining — not fixed in advance.\n4\nOutput bias terms\nBias parameters wk0 absorb the gap between average basis \nactivation and average target.\nTrade-off\nEvery one of these changes buys efficiency and better generalisation \n— but it also gives up the clean, exact, closed-form guarantees of the \noriginal interpolation network.\nResult: centres, widths, AND output weights\nall now need to be learned from data —\nwhich is exactly what the next lecture covers.\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n13\nC O M P A R I S O N\nRBF Network vs. Multi-Layer Perceptron\nAspect\nMLP\nRBF Network\nHidden layers\nOne or more\nExactly one\nHidden activation\nSigmoid / tanh (global)\nGaussian (local)\nDecision regions\nFormed by hyperplanes\nFormed by hyperspheres\nTraining\nAll weights via backprop, jointly\nTwo separate stages\nTypical training speed\nSlower (iterative, thousands of epochs)\nMuch faster (closed-form output stage)\nStrength\nGlobal function approximation, extrapolates reasonably\nFast, accurate local interpolation\nWeakness\nSlower to train\nPoor extrapolation; curse of dimensionality\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n16\nI N P R A C T I C E\nWhere RBF Networks Are Used\nFunction approximation\nModelling smooth continuous \nrelationships from sparse samples.\nPattern classification\nLocal receptive fields act as natural \nclass-region detectors.\nTime-series prediction\nLocal interpolation captures nearby \ndynamics well.\nControl systems\nFast training suits online / adaptive \ncontrol settings.\nQuick Summary\n•  RBF networks come from interpolation theory, not biology.\n•  Exact interpolation (one unit per point) is elegant but noisy and costly.\n•  Fewer, learned centres and widths make it practical — at the cost of exactness.\n•  Two-stage training (unsupervised centres, then linear output) makes RBF nets fast to train.\nRadial Basis Function Networks\n17\nN e x t L e c t u r e\nDetermining μj, σj, and wkj —\nHow RBF Networks Actually Learn\nSuggested reading\nBishop  — Sections 5.1–5.4\nHaykin  — Sections 5.1–5.4\nGurney  — Section 10.4\nCallan  — Section 2.6\nHertz, Krogh & Palmer  — Section 9.7\nBased on: John A. Bullinaria (2004), Introduction to Neural Networks — Lecture 12\n','Active','2026-08-21 11:57:06'),(9,'Test','testststtss',27,35,'04 Radial Basis Function Neural Netowork_new.pdf','I N T R O D U C T I O N T O N E U R A L N E T W O R K S\nRadial Basis Function\nNetworks\nA local, two-stage alter